      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For a general residential lawn in a place like Portland, you'll want a balanced fertilizer, but you can't go too heavy-handed, especially if your soil is already somewhat rich. A ratio like 10-10-10 or something similar is a safe starting point, but if your lawn is looking thin, you might lean slightly higher on the Nitrogen (N) to encourage more green growth, perhaps something like 20-5-10. Always read the application rate instructions on the bag—it's much better to under-fertilize slightly than to over-fertilize and burn your grass. Remember, even the best fertilizer is useless if the soil itself is compacted or poorly draining.",
